  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Cube Identities and Special Sums",
    "description": "Use (a ± b)^3, a^3 ± b^3 factorisations, and the a^3 + b^3 + c^3 = 3abc condition to crack heavy-looking expressions.",
    "teachingApproach": "Mirror the square-identity lesson one level up: cubing near 100, the two cancellation fractions (with the sign-flip warning), the 3abc condition that solves equations in one line, decimal expansions that collapse to 1, and running cube identities backwards from a ± b and ab. Fresh question and parallel retry after every idea.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 28,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 4, Simplification. Approved scope: Teach the cube identities the same way as the square ones: (a + b)^3 = a^3 + b^3 + 3ab(a + b) for cubing numbers near 100, and (a − b)^3 likewise. Show a^3 + b^3 = (a + b)(a^2 − ab + b^2) and a^3 − b^3 = (a − b)(a^2 + ab + b^2) as the tool for fraction expressions such as (343^3 − 113^3)/(343^2 + 343 × 113 + 113^2) = 230 where the whole denominator cancels. Teach the special condition a^3 + b^3 + c^3 = 3abc ⇒ a + b + c = 0 and use it on items such as (x − 4)^3 + (x − 9)^3 + (x − 8)^3 = 3(x − 4)(x − 9)(x − 8). Include decimal items such as (0.98)^3 + (0.02)^3 + 3 × 0.98 × 0.02 = 1 where the learner spots that the parts sum to 1, and finding a^3 ± b^3 from a ± b and ab. Learners should identify which identity shape an expression matches before computing anything."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's conquer the cube family.\n\nThe formulas look heavy, but each one exists to cancel something ugly. By the end you'll solve lines like (x − 4)^3 + (x − 9)^3 + (x − 8)^3 = 3(x − 4)(x − 9)(x − 8) in your head.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Cubes look scary. 101^3 means 101 × 101 × 101.\n\nBut cubes have the same shortcut family as squares:\n\n(a + b)^3 = a^3 + b^3 + 3ab(a + b)",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Watch it tame 101^3:\n\n= 100^3 + 1^3 + 3 × 100 × 1 × (100 + 1)\n= 1000000 + 1 + 30300\n= 1030301\n\nThe new player is 3ab(a + b) — three times the product times the total.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "q_1": {
